rs.\n\nBased on what I have, I'm organizing these into two lists: one ranked by mortality excluding pancreatic cancer, and another arranged by incidence starting with the least common. For the survival rates, lung cancer sits at roughly 28%, colorectal around 65%, liver and esophageal both near 22%, and breast cancer approximately 90%. I'll now draft the response using these figures.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "text", "text": "Now I have enough information. Let me write the response to answer.txt with the compiled data.\n\n"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_5b2976433458499cab562599", "name": "write", "arguments": {"path": "answer.txt", "content": "Cancer survival rates in the United States vary significantly depending on the type of cancer, stage at diagnosis, and available treatments. The overall 5-year relative survival rate for all cancers combined has improved from 49% in the mid-1970s to 69% in recent years, though this masks wide variation between cancer types. Cancers detected early tend to have much better outcomes—thyroid cancer reaches 99% survival when caught at an early stage, while some aggressive forms remain among the most difficult to treat effectively.\n\n• Lung cancer (28%)\n• Colorectal cancer (65%)\n• Breast cancer (90%)\n• Liver cancer (22%)\n• Esophageal cancer (22%)\n\n• Ewing sarcoma (~200 cases annually)\n• Malignant mesothelioma (~2,700 cases annually)\n• Soft tissue sarcoma (~13,500 cases annually)\n• Primary brain/CNS cancer (~24,000 cases annually)\n• Multiple myeloma (~35,800 cases annually)\n"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "modelstudio", "model": "qwen3.5-35b-a3b", "usage": {"input": 100557, "output": 1569, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 102126, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1776050161986, "responseId": "chatcmpl-80bf94d9-ce0e-9bc4-a932-791ad6b8d0cd"}}
